<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n5173712764> rdfs:label "Negative Pressure Ventilation and Positive Pressure Ventilation Promote Comparable Levels of Ventilator-Induced Diaphragmatic Dysfunction in Rats" ;
    ns6:abstract "Mechanical ventilation is a life-saving intervention for patients with respiratory failure. Unfortunately, a major complication associated with prolonged mechanical ventilation is ventilator-induced diaphragmatic atrophy and contractile dysfunction, termed ventilator-induced diaphragmatic dysfunction (VIDD). Emerging evidence suggests that positive pressure ventilation (PPV) promotes lung damage (ventilator-induced lung injury [VILI]), resulting in the release of signaling molecules that foster atrophic signaling in the diaphragm and the resultant VIDD. Although a recent report suggests that negative pressure ventilation (NPV) results in less VILI than PPV, it is unknown whether NPV can protect against VIDD. Therefore, the authors tested the hypothesis that compared with PPV, NPV will result in a lower level of VIDD." ;
    ns6:pageStart "652" ;
    ns6:volume "119" ;
    ns3:grantCited "R01 HL780839" ;
    ns3:harvestedBy "Python Pubs version 1.1" ;
