    ns1:pmcid "PMC3368880" .

<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n545501496> rdfs:label "Minimal Change Disease in Graft Versus Host Disease: a Podocyte Response To the Graft?" ;
    ns6:abstract "Nephrotic syndrome is a rare complication of hematopoietic cell transplantation. It has been suggested that nephrotic syndrome may represent a limited form of graft-versus-host disease although the pathological link between these two entities remains unclear. In this paper, we report a case of a 61-year-old female who underwent nonmyeloablative allogenic stem cell transplantation for T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia and subsequently developed biopsy proven minimal change disease shortly after cessation of her immunosuppression therapy. Urinary CD80 was markedly elevated during active disease and disappeared following corticosteroid-induced remission. We hypothesize that alloreactive donor T cells target the kidney and induce podocyte expression of CD80 that results in proteinuria from limited 'graft versus host' disease." ;
    ns6:number "6" ;
    ns6:pageStart "469" ;
    ns6:volume "80" ;
    ns3:grantCited "DK-80764" ;
