<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n6615213655> rdfs:label "Reduced Cerebral Blood Flow and White Matter Hyperintensities Predict Poor Sleep in Heart Failure" ;
    ns6:abstract "Poor sleep is common in heart failure (HF), though mechanisms of sleep difficulties are not well understood. Adverse brain changes among regions important for sleep have been demonstrated in patients with HF. Cerebral hypoperfusion, a correlate of sleep quality, is also prevalent in HF and a likely contributor to white matter hyperintensities (WMH). However, no study to date has examined the effects of cerebral blood flow, WMH, and brain volume on sleep quality in HF." ;
    ns6:volume "9" ;
    ns3:harvestedBy "Python Pubs version 1.3" ;
