        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n9986459385> ;
    ns4:title "A Computational Model of the Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Gonadal Axis in Female Fathead Minnows (Pimephales Promelas) Exposed To 17 Alpha-Ethynylestradiol and 17 Beta-Trenbolone" ;
    ns6:abstract "Endocrine disrupting chemicals (e.g., estrogens, androgens and their mimics) are known to affect reproduction in fish. 17α-ethynylestradiol is a synthetic estrogen used in birth control pills. 17β-trenbolone is a relatively stable metabolite of trenbolone acetate, a synthetic androgen used as a growth promoter in livestock. Both 17α-ethynylestradiol and 17β-trenbolone have been found in the aquatic environment and affect fish reproduction. In this study, we developed a physiologically-based computational model for female fathead minnows (FHM, Pimephales promelas), a small fish species used in ecotoxicology, to simulate how estrogens (i.e., 17α-ethynylestradiol) or androgens (i.e., 17β-trenbolone) affect reproductive endpoints such as plasma concentrations of steroid hormones (e.g., 17β-estradiol and testosterone) and vitellogenin (a precursor to egg yolk proteins)." ;
    ns6:volume "5" ;
    ns3:harvestedBy "Python Pubs version 1.1" ;
    ns1:informationResourceInAuthorship <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1022421189>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1484441222>,
